==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{m|enm|therwith}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|þǣrwiþ||therewith, in return, in exchange, against
}}, equivalent to {{compound|en|there|with}}. Cognate with {{cog|da|derved||therewith}}, {{cog|sv|därvid||therewith, thereby}}. Parallel with Dutch {{m|nl|daarmee}}, German {{m|de|damit||therewith}}, though etymologically different. See also {{l|en|theremid}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ðɛəˈwɪθ/|/ðɛəˈwɪð/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|En-us-therewith.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv|-}}

# [[with|With]] this, that or those.
#: '''ca. 1370:''' He ȝaue ȝow fyue wittes//For to wershepen hym '''ther-with''' - ''[[w:Piers Plowman|Piers Plowman]]'', ii-16
#: I have learned, in whatever state I am, therewith to be content. - ''Philippians'', 4:11
# In [[addition]] to that; besides, moreover.
#: To speak of strength and '''therewith''' hardiness. - [[w:Geoffrey Chaucer|Geoffrey Chaucer]]
# [[thereupon|Thereupon]], [[forthwith]]; with that being said or done.
#: '''1869:''' 'I take the privilege, Mistress Ruth, of saluting you.' ...And '''therewith''' I bussed her well. - [[w:Richard Blackmore|Richard Blackmore]], ''Lorna Doone'', 1.

